1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,810
A Japanese saw cuts as
you pull it toward you.

2
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:08,070
That one fact shapes much of the tool.

3
00:00:08,733 --> 00:00:09,733
This time:

4
00:00:09,913 --> 00:00:15,483
the saws, and exactly where the
cut goes in relation to the line.

5
00:00:21,267 --> 00:00:24,557
Most Western saws cut on the push stroke.

6
00:00:25,147 --> 00:00:27,257
Japanese saws cut on the pull.

7
00:00:27,887 --> 00:00:32,037
Pulling puts the blade in tension,
so it can be made thinner.

8
00:00:32,667 --> 00:00:37,037
A pushed blade generally needs to be
stiffer, so that it doesn't buckle.

9
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:41,317
Every saw cut has a
width, called the kerf.

10
00:00:41,787 --> 00:00:47,597
A thinner blade usually makes a narrower
kerf, so there is less wood to remove.

11
00:00:51,767 --> 00:00:56,607
A common carpenter's saw is the
ryōba, which means double-edged.

12
00:00:57,167 --> 00:01:02,057
One edge rips, cutting along
the grain; that's tatebiki.

13
00:01:02,607 --> 00:01:07,237
The other crosscuts, cutting
across it; that's yokobiki.

14
00:01:07,907 --> 00:01:10,077
The design is fairly modern:

15
00:01:10,447 --> 00:01:13,919
it dates from the Meiji era,
in the late eighteen hundreds.

16
00:01:17,733 --> 00:01:19,743
Look closely at the teeth.

17
00:01:20,413 --> 00:01:25,783
Rip teeth work like a row of tiny
chisels, paring along the fibers.

18
00:01:25,938 --> 00:01:33,023
Crosscut teeth are pointed like small
knives, and sever the fibers one by one.

19
00:01:37,067 --> 00:01:40,137
For fine joinery there is the dōzuki.

20
00:01:40,527 --> 00:01:45,557
Its blade is very thin, so a metal
spine along the back keeps it stiff.

21
00:01:46,087 --> 00:01:48,317
It is classed as a crosscut saw.

22
00:01:48,813 --> 00:01:55,183
Dōzuki is also the word for a tenon's
shoulder, the face around its base.

23
00:01:55,813 --> 00:01:58,443
Cutting it is a clean crosscut:

24
00:01:58,873 --> 00:02:01,443
exactly the kind of work this saw does.

25
00:02:04,967 --> 00:02:10,617
There is also the kataba, a saw
with a single edge and no spine.

26
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:15,353
Look at the kerf end-on.

27
00:02:15,813 --> 00:02:19,403
The teeth are bent out
alternately, left and right.

28
00:02:20,053 --> 00:02:23,043
This is called the set, or asari.

29
00:02:23,713 --> 00:02:26,993
So the kerf is a little
wider than the blade, and

30
00:02:27,033 --> 00:02:29,663
that width is why the
next question matters.

31
00:02:33,867 --> 00:02:37,377
Where does the kerf go,
relative to the line?

32
00:02:37,987 --> 00:02:43,497
The carpenter chooses, and the skills
test manual sets out three options.

33
00:02:44,227 --> 00:02:45,697
In English shop talk:

34
00:02:46,007 --> 00:02:51,507
you can take the line, cutting it
away; leave the line, sawing just on

35
00:02:51,547 --> 00:02:56,317
the waste side; or split it, with
the kerf centered on the line.

36
00:02:56,833 --> 00:03:00,703
The choice depends on how
tight each joint should be.

37
00:03:01,053 --> 00:03:04,763
Some fits should be snug,
and some a little looser.

38
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:14,070
The national skills test manual
for carpenters gives an example.

39
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:20,930
On one tenon, the thickness cuts split
the line, so it fits a little looser.

40
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:24,620
The width cuts leave
the line, with the saw

41
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:28,450
just on the waste side, so it fits tight.

42
00:03:28,867 --> 00:03:31,897
Either way, the line is the reference.

43
00:03:32,427 --> 00:03:34,937
That's why the marking
out had to be exact.

44
00:03:39,233 --> 00:03:43,543
If you own Western saws,
the same ideas carry over:

45
00:03:43,933 --> 00:03:48,123
rip and crosscut teeth,
a backed saw for joinery.

46
00:03:48,693 --> 00:03:50,843
The big difference is the stroke.

47
00:03:53,733 --> 00:03:55,783
Next time, the rest of the kit:

48
00:03:56,333 --> 00:03:59,323
the chisels, the hammer, and the plane.

49
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:01,563
Pull to cut.

50
00:04:02,093 --> 00:04:05,903
Rip along the grain, crosscut across it.

51
00:04:06,053 --> 00:04:09,063
And before every cut, decide:

52
00:04:09,613 --> 00:04:13,203
take the line, leave it, or split it.

53
00:04:22,967 --> 00:04:24,037
One note:

54
00:04:24,107 --> 00:04:28,847
sharp-tool technique, and how
to use these tools safely, is

55
00:04:28,887 --> 00:04:32,537
best learned from a teacher,
at a bench, in person.
